patch: reject a negative -p count.
patch accepted a negative -p count, such as -p-1, though it has no
meaning: the name is never shortened to that many components, so the
file to patch is never opened. patch then went on to apply each hunk
to descriptor -1. The read fails at once, so the hunk fails, and
fail_hunk() dereferenced the NULL name of the temporary file it never
created: "patch -p-1" crashed.

Parse the count with xatoi_positive() so that a negative count is
rejected up front with "invalid number", as GNU patch rejects it too.

---
 editors/patch.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/patch.c
+++ b/editors/patch.c
@@ -409,7 +409,7 @@
 
 	argv += optind;
 	reverse = opts & FLAG_REVERSE;
-	TT.prefix = (opts & FLAG_PATHLEN) ? xatoi(opt_p) : 0; // can be negative!
+	TT.prefix = (opts & FLAG_PATHLEN) ? xatoi_positive(opt_p) : 0;
 	TT.filein = TT.fileout = -1;
 	if (opts & FLAG_INPUT) {
 		xmove_fd(xopen_stdin(opt_i), STDIN_FILENO);
